# Overview of the Mirror Strategy

We will use GitHub as the primary repo and automatically mirror it to GitLab.


GitHub (primary)
│
│ push
▼
GitHub Action
│
▼
GitLab (mirror)


Benefits:

- Automatic sync on every push
- Keeps GitLab fully up-to-date
- No manual work
- Works for public or private repos

Step 1 — Create the GitLab repository

Log into GitLab  
Click New Project  
Choose Blank Project  

Important settings:  
- Project name: same as GitHub repo  
- Visibility: your choice  
- Initialize repository: ❌ DO NOT initialize  

You want the repo completely empty.  

Example:  

gitlab.com/username/project-name


Step 2 — Create a GitLab Access Token

In GitLab:  
Settings → Access Tokens  

Create a token with permissions:  
- write_repository  
- read_repository  

Copy the token — you’ll need it shortly.

Step 3 — Add the token to GitHub

Go to your repo on GitHub.  
Settings → Secrets and Variables → Actions → New Repository Secret  

Create:  
- Name: GITLAB_TOKEN  
- Value: (your GitLab token)

Step 4 — Add the mirror workflow

Create this file in your GitHub repo:  

.github/workflows/gitlab-mirror.yml


Use this workflow:

```yaml
name: Mirror to GitLab

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main

jobs:
  mirror:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0

      - name: Push to GitLab
        run: |
          git remote add gitlab https://oauth2:${{ secrets.GITLAB_TOKEN }}@gitlab.com/VictoryTek/YOUR_REPO.git
          git push gitlab --all
          git push gitlab --tags

Replace:

YOUR_REPO

Step 5 — Push the workflow

git add .github/workflows/gitlab-mirror.yml
git commit -m "Add GitLab mirror workflow"
git push

Step 6 — Verify the mirror

Once the push finishes:

Go to GitHub Actions and confirm the workflow ran successfully

Open your GitLab repo

You should now see the full repository mirrored

Optional — Mirror ALL branches

Change this section:

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main

to:

on:
  push:

This mirrors every branch automatically.

Optional — Reverse mirror (GitLab → GitHub)

GitLab also supports native repository mirroring:
Settings → Repository → Mirroring Repositories

You can mirror GitLab back to GitHub, creating a two-way sync.

⚠️ Warning: Two-way mirrors can conflict, so most developers prefer one-way mirroring:
GitHub → GitLab

What this setup gives you:

✔ Automatic backup of all repos

✔ Protection if one platform has issues

✔ Independence from platform policies

✔ Access to both ecosystems

✔ CI/CD flexibility

Bonus tip (very powerful):

You can create one reusable workflow and copy it to all repos, or use organization-level automation.

Some developers even mirror to multiple platforms for maximum resilience:

GitHub
GitLab
Codeberg
Self-hosted Gitea